WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.416
This is part two of the property relations samplex quiz

00:00:04.416 --> 00:00:08.832
for Attorney Uribe's class: twenty-seven samplex questions on the absolute

00:00:08.832 --> 00:00:13.248
community, the conjugal partnership of gains, and separation of property.

00:00:13.408 --> 00:00:16.686
Each question is read aloud, then you get five seconds

00:00:16.686 --> 00:00:19.637
to answer before the reveal and a short explanation.

00:00:19.797 --> 00:00:22.720
A statement is true only if it's absolutely true.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:23.840
Let's begin.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.104
Part II: Absolute Community.

00:00:27.864 --> 00:00:28.888
Question 1.

00:00:29.048 --> 00:00:30.115
True or false?

00:00:30.275 --> 00:00:33.224
The community property shall consist of all the property

00:00:33.224 --> 00:00:36.173
owned by the spouses at the time of the

00:00:36.173 --> 00:00:38.467
celebration of the marriage or acquired thereafter.

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:45.714
The answer is false.

00:00:46.374 --> 00:00:49.745
Article ninety-one begins, unless otherwise provided.

00:00:49.905 --> 00:00:54.492
Article ninety-two excludes property acquired during the marriage by gratuitous title,

00:00:54.492 --> 00:00:59.080
property for personal and exclusive use except jewelry, and property of

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:02.833
a spouse with legitimate descendants by a former marriage.

00:01:02.993 --> 00:01:04.870
Sir wants that last one mentioned too.

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:07.211
Question 2.

00:01:07.371 --> 00:01:08.438
True or false?

00:01:08.598 --> 00:01:11.830
The community property shall consist of all the property owned

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:15.062
by the spouses at the time of celebration of marriage.

00:01:21.072 --> 00:01:22.309
The answer is false.

00:01:22.969 --> 00:01:27.562
Article ninety-one says property owned at the celebration or acquired thereafter,

00:01:27.562 --> 00:01:31.737
and even that is subject to the Article ninety-two exclusions.

00:01:33.097 --> 00:01:34.121
Question 3.

00:01:34.281 --> 00:01:36.734
This shall be excluded from the community property:

00:01:37.195 --> 00:01:41.568
A: Property acquired during the marriage by onerous title by either spouse.

00:01:41.978 --> 00:01:46.714
B: Property acquired before the marriage by gratuitous title by either spouse.

00:01:47.124 --> 00:01:50.473
C: Jewelry for the personal and exclusive use of a spouse.

00:01:50.883 --> 00:01:52.270
D: All of the above.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.045
E: None of the above.

00:02:00.055 --> 00:02:02.124
The answer is E: none of the above.

00:02:02.785 --> 00:02:06.732
Property acquired by onerous title during the marriage is community.

00:02:06.892 --> 00:02:11.585
Property owned before the marriage is community however acquired, because

00:02:11.585 --> 00:02:15.809
the gratuitous-title exclusion covers only acquisitions during the marriage.

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:18.252
And jewelry is always community property.

00:02:19.611 --> 00:02:20.678
Question 4.

00:02:20.838 --> 00:02:23.291
This shall be excluded from the community property:

00:02:23.751 --> 00:02:28.124
A: Property acquired during the marriage by onerous title by either spouse.

00:02:28.535 --> 00:02:32.034
B: Property acquired during the marriage by gratuitous title.

00:02:32.443 --> 00:02:37.072
C: Property acquired before the marriage by gratuitous title by either spouse.

00:02:37.483 --> 00:02:40.150
D: Jewelry for personal and exclusive use.

00:02:40.559 --> 00:02:41.924
E: None of the above.

00:02:47.935 --> 00:02:50.004
The answer is E: none of the above.

00:02:50.664 --> 00:02:51.709
B is the trap.

00:02:51.869 --> 00:02:55.443
Gratuitous acquisitions during the marriage are excluded unless the

00:02:55.443 --> 00:02:59.018
donor or testator provides that they form part of

00:02:59.018 --> 00:03:02.195
the community, so B isn't absolutely an exclusion.

00:03:02.355 --> 00:03:04.125
The rest are community property.

00:03:05.485 --> 00:03:06.552
Question 5.

00:03:06.712 --> 00:03:08.849
A parcel of land is presumed to

00:03:08.849 --> 00:03:10.680
belong to the community property if:

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:13.615
A: it was acquired by gratuitous title.

00:03:14.025 --> 00:03:16.308
B: it was acquired by onerous title.

00:03:16.717 --> 00:03:18.872
C: it was acquired before the marriage.

00:03:19.282 --> 00:03:21.522
D: it was registered during the marriage.

00:03:21.932 --> 00:03:23.297
E: none of the above.

00:03:29.307 --> 00:03:31.376
The answer is E: none of the above.

00:03:32.037 --> 00:03:35.418
Under Article ninety-three, the presumption is triggered by acquisition

00:03:35.418 --> 00:03:38.800
during the marriage, and that isn't among the choices.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:43.856
The title doesn't matter, property owned before the marriage is community

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:48.752
by Article ninety-one rather than by presumption, and registration isn't acquisition.

00:03:50.111 --> 00:03:51.199
Question 6.

00:03:51.359 --> 00:03:54.922
A parcel of land is presumed to belong to the community if:

00:03:55.382 --> 00:03:57.537
A: Acquired by gratuitous title.

00:03:57.947 --> 00:03:59.974
B: Acquired by onerous title.

00:04:00.383 --> 00:04:02.303
C: Acquired before the marriage.

00:04:02.713 --> 00:04:04.612
D: Acquired during the marriage.

00:04:05.022 --> 00:04:06.942
E: Registered during the marriage.

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:15.640
The answer is D: acquired during the marriage.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:21.036
Article ninety-three presumes property acquired during the marriage to be community.

00:04:21.196 --> 00:04:23.671
Compare the last question: when that choice is

00:04:23.671 --> 00:04:26.145
missing, the answer is none of the above.

00:04:27.505 --> 00:04:28.593
Question 7.

00:04:28.753 --> 00:04:31.206
This shall be excluded from the community property:

00:04:31.667 --> 00:04:36.040
A: Property acquired during the marriage by onerous title by either spouse.

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:39.759
B: Property acquired before the marriage by either spouse

00:04:39.759 --> 00:04:42.701
who has legitimate descendants by a former marriage.

00:04:43.111 --> 00:04:47.740
C: Property acquired before the marriage by gratuitous title by either spouse.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:49.387
D: Jewelry.

00:04:49.797 --> 00:04:51.162
E: None of the above.

00:04:57.173 --> 00:04:58.325
The answer is B.

00:04:58.985 --> 00:05:01.758
That's verbatim Article ninety-two, number three.

00:05:01.918 --> 00:05:05.118
It protects the legitimes of the children of the first marriage.

00:05:05.278 --> 00:05:07.582
The other choices are community property.

00:05:08.942 --> 00:05:09.966
Question 8.

00:05:10.126 --> 00:05:12.579
This shall be excluded from the community property:

00:05:13.039 --> 00:05:17.412
A: Property acquired during the marriage by onerous title by either spouse.

00:05:17.823 --> 00:05:20.669
B: Property acquired before the marriage by either spouse

00:05:20.669 --> 00:05:23.199
who has a child by a prior marriage.

00:05:23.609 --> 00:05:28.238
C: Property acquired before the marriage by gratuitous title by either spouse.

00:05:28.648 --> 00:05:31.315
D: Jewelry for personal and exclusive use.

00:05:31.725 --> 00:05:33.090
E: None of the above.

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.252
The answer is B.

00:05:40.912 --> 00:05:44.413
Sir accepted it: a child by a prior marriage

00:05:44.413 --> 00:05:47.525
is legitimate, so Article ninety-two, number three applies.

00:05:47.685 --> 00:05:51.269
Jewelry is community property even if it's for personal use.

00:05:52.629 --> 00:05:53.674
Question 9.

00:05:53.834 --> 00:05:56.288
This shall be excluded from the community property:

00:05:56.748 --> 00:06:01.121
A: Property acquired during the marriage by onerous title by either spouse.

00:06:01.531 --> 00:06:04.205
B: Property acquired before marriage by either spouse

00:06:04.205 --> 00:06:06.544
who has a child before the marriage.

00:06:06.955 --> 00:06:11.584
C: Property acquired before the marriage by gratuitous title by either spouse.

00:06:11.994 --> 00:06:13.231
D: Jewelry.

00:06:13.641 --> 00:06:15.006
E: None of the above.

00:06:21.017 --> 00:06:23.086
The answer is E: none of the above.

00:06:23.746 --> 00:06:28.397
Article ninety-two, number three needs legitimate descendants by a former marriage.

00:06:28.557 --> 00:06:31.412
A child born before the marriage may be

00:06:31.412 --> 00:06:33.911
illegitimate, so B isn't absolutely an exclusion.

00:06:34.071 --> 00:06:36.288
Compare the last question, where a child

00:06:36.288 --> 00:06:38.189
by a prior marriage was accepted.

00:06:39.549 --> 00:06:40.594
Question 10.

00:06:40.754 --> 00:06:43.528
If not absolutely true, the statement is false.

00:06:43.987 --> 00:06:45.011
Statement one.

00:06:45.171 --> 00:06:49.022
A petition for the adoption under the Domestic Administrative Adoption Act

00:06:49.022 --> 00:06:52.874
may prosper even if the petitioner, who is an alien, has

00:06:52.874 --> 00:06:56.725
not resided in the Philippines for three years prior to the

00:06:56.725 --> 00:07:00.576
filing of the petition and the child to be adopted is

00:07:00.576 --> 00:07:03.027
an illegitimate child of his Filipino spouse.

00:07:03.487 --> 00:07:04.511
Statement two.

00:07:04.671 --> 00:07:07.721
In the absence of a marriage settlement, a parcel

00:07:07.721 --> 00:07:10.771
of land inherited by A during his marriage to

00:07:10.771 --> 00:07:13.482
B in 1989 will be his separate property.

00:07:13.942 --> 00:07:15.073
A: both are true.

00:07:15.233 --> 00:07:16.555
B: both are false.

00:07:16.715 --> 00:07:19.190
C: No. 1 is true, No. 2 is false.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:21.931
D: No. 1 is false, No. 2 is true.

00:07:27.941 --> 00:07:29.882
The answer is A: both are true.

00:07:30.543 --> 00:07:31.908
Both are true, per Sir.

00:07:32.068 --> 00:07:36.228
Statement one is an adoption point: the residency requirement can be

00:07:36.228 --> 00:07:40.388
waived for an alien adopting the child of a Filipino spouse.

00:07:40.548 --> 00:07:45.657
Statement two: land inherited by A during a nineteen eighty-nine marriage is

00:07:45.657 --> 00:07:50.340
excluded under Article ninety-two, number one, because only A inherited it.

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:52.852
Question 11.

00:07:53.012 --> 00:07:55.785
If not absolutely true, the statement is false.

00:07:56.245 --> 00:07:57.269
Statement one.

00:07:57.429 --> 00:08:00.343
The spouses will have joint parental authority if an

00:08:00.343 --> 00:08:02.933
illegitimate child of one of them is adopted.

00:08:03.393 --> 00:08:04.417
Statement two.

00:08:04.577 --> 00:08:07.628
The rentals from a building inherited by B during

00:08:07.628 --> 00:08:10.678
her marriage to A in 1989 will be a

00:08:10.678 --> 00:08:13.729
community property in the absence of a marriage settlement.

00:08:14.189 --> 00:08:15.320
A: both are true.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:16.802
B: both are false.

00:08:16.962 --> 00:08:19.437
C: No. 1 is true, No. 2 is false.

00:08:19.597 --> 00:08:22.178
D: No. 1 is false, No. 2 is true.

00:08:28.189 --> 00:08:30.344
The answer is B: both are false.

00:08:31.003 --> 00:08:32.048
Both are false.

00:08:32.208 --> 00:08:35.195
When one spouse adopts their own illegitimate child,

00:08:35.195 --> 00:08:38.182
the other only consents and isn't a co-adopter.

00:08:38.342 --> 00:08:42.310
And the inherited building is excluded under Article ninety-two together with

00:08:42.310 --> 00:08:46.278
its fruits and income, so the rentals are B's separate property.

00:08:47.638 --> 00:08:48.726
Question 12.

00:08:48.886 --> 00:08:51.659
If not absolutely true, the statement is false.

00:08:52.119 --> 00:08:53.143
Statement one.

00:08:53.303 --> 00:08:57.592
If X died in 1987, the marriage between X and Y may

00:08:57.592 --> 00:09:01.882
be valid even if there was no marriage license if they cohabited

00:09:01.882 --> 00:09:06.171
as husband and wife for six years prior to the marriage and

00:09:06.171 --> 00:09:09.388
their cohabitation was not entirely free from legal impediment.

00:09:09.849 --> 00:09:10.873
Statement two.

00:09:11.033 --> 00:09:15.577
The absolute community of property shall be liable for all expenses for

00:09:15.577 --> 00:09:20.121
mere preservation made during marriage upon the separate property of either spouse.

00:09:20.581 --> 00:09:21.712
A: both are true.

00:09:21.872 --> 00:09:23.194
B: both are false.

00:09:23.354 --> 00:09:25.829
C: No. 1 is true, No. 2 is false.

00:09:25.989 --> 00:09:28.570
D: No. 1 is false, No. 2 is true.

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:36.735
The answer is B: both are false.

00:09:37.395 --> 00:09:38.440
Both are false.

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:41.022
Under the Civil Code rule, the cohabitation

00:09:41.022 --> 00:09:43.443
must have been free of legal impediment.

00:09:43.603 --> 00:09:47.091
And Article ninety-four, number five covers preservation expenses

00:09:47.091 --> 00:09:50.579
only for separate property used by the family.

00:09:50.739 --> 00:09:52.958
Sir: the term is used, not benefit.

00:09:54.317 --> 00:09:55.576
Question 13.

00:09:55.736 --> 00:09:58.731
In the will of X, a parcel of land

00:09:58.731 --> 00:10:01.726
was validly bequeathed to A who was married to

00:10:01.726 --> 00:10:04.056
B at 5PM on June 1, 2019.

00:10:04.216 --> 00:10:07.544
A and B orally agreed for a conjugal partnership of gains.

00:10:07.704 --> 00:10:11.245
At 10AM on June 1, 2019, X died.

00:10:11.405 --> 00:10:13.752
This parcel of land will form part of the:

00:10:14.212 --> 00:10:16.281
A: conjugal partnership property.

00:10:16.691 --> 00:10:18.270
B: community property.

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:20.365
C: separate property of A.

00:10:20.775 --> 00:10:22.055
D: estate of X.

00:10:22.465 --> 00:10:23.937
E: property of the State.

00:10:29.947 --> 00:10:32.208
The answer is B: community property.

00:10:32.869 --> 00:10:35.770
A owned the land from ten a.m., when X died.

00:10:35.930 --> 00:10:40.761
The oral agreement is void, so the regime is absolute community, which

00:10:40.761 --> 00:10:45.189
began at five p.m. and covers property owned at the celebration.

00:10:45.349 --> 00:10:48.117
The land was acquired before the marriage,

00:10:48.117 --> 00:10:50.490
so the gratuitous-title exclusion doesn't apply.

00:10:51.850 --> 00:10:53.045
Question 14.

00:10:53.205 --> 00:10:57.450
A inherited a parcel of land from his grandfather in 1989.

00:10:57.610 --> 00:11:00.383
Thereafter, A married B without a marriage settlement.

00:11:00.543 --> 00:11:04.502
In 1995, A died intestate survived by B; C, their son;

00:11:04.502 --> 00:11:08.462
D and E, his children with his girlfriend Y before his

00:11:08.462 --> 00:11:11.701
marriage to B; and F and G, his parents.

00:11:11.861 --> 00:11:14.549
Is the land the separate or exclusive property of A?

00:11:15.009 --> 00:11:18.380
A: Yes, because it was acquired by gratuitous title.

00:11:18.789 --> 00:11:21.776
B: Yes, because A has children by another woman.

00:11:22.186 --> 00:11:24.917
C: No, it is community property of A and B.

00:11:25.327 --> 00:11:28.079
D: No, it is co-owned by A and his children.

00:11:34.089 --> 00:11:36.134
The answer is C: no, it is

00:11:36.134 --> 00:11:37.886
community property of A and B.

00:11:38.546 --> 00:11:43.365
The marriage came after nineteen eighty-eight with no settlement, so it's absolute

00:11:43.365 --> 00:11:47.783
community, and property owned at the celebration is community however acquired.

00:11:47.943 --> 00:11:50.826
A's children with Y are illegitimate, so

00:11:50.826 --> 00:11:53.298
Article ninety-two, number three doesn't apply.

00:11:53.458 --> 00:11:55.378
Sir said ninety percent got this wrong.

00:11:56.738 --> 00:11:57.911
Question 15.

00:11:58.071 --> 00:12:01.869
Bong and Liza were married on September 2, 1987.

00:12:02.029 --> 00:12:04.901
Bong died on August 2, 1989, survived

00:12:04.901 --> 00:12:07.362
by Liza and their daughter Imee.

00:12:07.522 --> 00:12:10.338
Thereafter, Liza married Rudy; they orally agreed

00:12:10.338 --> 00:12:13.154
to adopt the conjugal partnership of gains.

00:12:13.314 --> 00:12:16.386
Before her marriage to Rudy, Liza had inherited from her

00:12:16.386 --> 00:12:19.458
father a parcel of land that was being rented out.

00:12:19.618 --> 00:12:22.967
A year after the solemnization of the marriage, Liza died.

00:12:23.127 --> 00:12:26.580
Will the rentals, from the solemnization of the marriage of Liza

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:29.719
and Rudy until her death, form part of her estate?

00:12:30.179 --> 00:12:34.318
A: No, because the rentals are community property of Liza and Rudy.

00:12:34.728 --> 00:12:38.419
B: No, because the rentals are conjugal partnership property.

00:12:38.829 --> 00:12:42.615
C: Yes, because the land and its fruits are excluded from

00:12:42.615 --> 00:12:46.402
the community, Liza having a legitimate descendant by a former marriage.

00:12:46.812 --> 00:12:50.417
D: Yes, because inherited property is always exclusive.

00:12:56.427 --> 00:12:57.579
The answer is C.

00:12:58.239 --> 00:13:02.236
Liza and Rudy married after nineteen eighty-nine, and their oral

00:13:02.236 --> 00:13:05.834
agreement is void, so the regime is absolute community.

00:13:05.994 --> 00:13:09.315
Liza's land would ordinarily be community, but she has

00:13:09.315 --> 00:13:12.635
a legitimate daughter by a former marriage, so Article

00:13:12.635 --> 00:13:15.956
ninety-two, number three excludes the land and its fruits.

00:13:16.116 --> 00:13:17.780
The rentals go to her estate.

00:13:19.141 --> 00:13:21.466
Part III: Conjugal Partnership.

00:13:22.226 --> 00:13:23.421
Question 16.

00:13:23.581 --> 00:13:24.647
True or false?

00:13:24.807 --> 00:13:28.820
The conjugal partnership shall be governed by the rules of co-ownership in

00:13:28.820 --> 00:13:32.834
all that is not in conflict with what is expressly determined in

00:13:32.834 --> 00:13:36.178
this Chapter or by the spouses in their marriage settlements.

00:13:42.188 --> 00:13:43.425
The answer is false.

00:13:44.085 --> 00:13:47.818
Article one oh eight says the rules on the contract of partnership.

00:13:47.978 --> 00:13:52.928
Co-ownership is the suppletory law for the absolute community, under Article ninety.

00:13:54.288 --> 00:13:55.568
Question 17.

00:13:55.728 --> 00:13:59.888
The conjugal partnership shall be liable for all debts and

00:13:59.888 --> 00:14:04.048
obligations contracted during the marriage by the designated administrator-spouse for

00:14:04.048 --> 00:14:07.376
the benefit of the conjugal partnership of gains.

00:14:07.536 --> 00:14:11.504
In case of insufficiency of said assets, the spouses shall be liable:

00:14:11.964 --> 00:14:13.201
A: Solidarily.

00:14:13.611 --> 00:14:14.806
B: Jointly.

00:14:15.216 --> 00:14:16.624
C: Alternatively.

00:14:17.034 --> 00:14:18.399
D: Cumulatively.

00:14:24.409 --> 00:14:26.457
The answer is A: solidarily.

00:14:27.117 --> 00:14:31.725
Under Article one twenty-one, if the conjugal partnership is insufficient, the

00:14:31.725 --> 00:14:36.333
spouses are solidarily liable for the balance with their separate properties.

00:14:36.493 --> 00:14:39.394
The same rule applies to the absolute community.

00:14:39.554 --> 00:14:41.005
Jointly was the old rule.

00:14:42.365 --> 00:14:43.517
Question 18.

00:14:43.677 --> 00:14:45.619
The fruits from the exclusive property of

00:14:45.619 --> 00:14:47.560
a spouse shall form part of the:

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:52.436
A: exclusive properties of a spouse in a conjugal partnership of gains.

00:14:52.846 --> 00:14:55.890
B: conjugal partnership properties only if the

00:14:55.890 --> 00:14:58.499
property was acquired by gratuitous title.

00:14:58.909 --> 00:15:03.282
C: community properties if property was acquired by gratuitous title.

00:15:03.693 --> 00:15:07.896
D: community properties if property was acquired by onerous title.

00:15:08.305 --> 00:15:09.670
E: none of the above.

00:15:15.681 --> 00:15:17.750
The answer is E: none of the above.

00:15:18.410 --> 00:15:22.431
In a conjugal partnership, the fruits of exclusive property are conjugal

00:15:22.431 --> 00:15:26.453
regardless of how it was acquired, so A and B fail.

00:15:26.613 --> 00:15:29.640
In the absolute community, fruits of excluded property stay

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:32.330
with the property, so C and D fail.

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:34.885
Question 19.

00:15:35.045 --> 00:15:38.917
In a conjugal partnership of gains, without a marriage settlement, the rentals

00:15:38.917 --> 00:15:42.789
from the exclusive property of a spouse shall form part of the:

00:15:43.249 --> 00:15:45.446
A: exclusive properties of a spouse.

00:15:45.856 --> 00:15:48.900
B: conjugal partnership properties only if the

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:51.509
property was acquired by gratuitous title.

00:15:51.919 --> 00:15:54.779
C: conjugal partnership properties only if the

00:15:54.779 --> 00:15:57.231
property was acquired by onerous title.

00:15:57.641 --> 00:16:02.036
D: community properties if property was acquired by gratuitous title.

00:16:02.446 --> 00:16:06.521
E: community properties if property was acquired by onerous title.

00:16:06.931 --> 00:16:08.275
F: none of the above.

00:16:14.285 --> 00:16:16.440
The answer is F: none of the above.

00:16:17.099 --> 00:16:21.547
Under Article one seventeen, number three, the net fruits of each

00:16:21.547 --> 00:16:25.590
spouse's exclusive property are conjugal, and rentals are civil fruits.

00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:29.601
The title doesn't matter, so the only-if choices fail, and

00:16:29.601 --> 00:16:33.067
community property doesn't belong in a conjugal partnership question.

00:16:33.227 --> 00:16:35.083
Sir called those the worst answers.

00:16:36.443 --> 00:16:37.552
Question 20.

00:16:37.712 --> 00:16:41.584
In a conjugal partnership of gains, without a marriage settlement, the rentals

00:16:41.584 --> 00:16:45.456
from the exclusive property of a spouse shall form part of the:

00:16:45.917 --> 00:16:48.114
A: exclusive properties of a spouse.

00:16:48.524 --> 00:16:53.409
B: conjugal properties only if the property was acquired by gratuitous title.

00:16:53.819 --> 00:16:58.512
C: conjugal properties only if the property was acquired by onerous title.

00:16:58.923 --> 00:17:03.126
D: community properties if property was acquired by onerous title.

00:17:03.535 --> 00:17:04.900
E: none of the above.

00:17:10.911 --> 00:17:12.980
The answer is E: none of the above.

00:17:13.640 --> 00:17:17.419
Rentals of exclusive property are conjugal under Article one

00:17:17.419 --> 00:17:21.197
seventeen, whatever the title, so the only-if choices fail,

00:17:21.197 --> 00:17:24.136
and community property is the wrong regime.

00:17:25.496 --> 00:17:26.797
Question 21.

00:17:26.957 --> 00:17:30.829
In a conjugal partnership of gains, without a marriage settlement, the rentals

00:17:30.829 --> 00:17:34.701
from the exclusive property of a spouse shall form part of the:

00:17:35.161 --> 00:17:37.358
A: exclusive properties of a spouse.

00:17:37.769 --> 00:17:40.813
B: conjugal partnership properties only if the

00:17:40.813 --> 00:17:43.422
property was acquired by gratuitous title.

00:17:43.832 --> 00:17:46.692
C: conjugal partnership properties only if the

00:17:46.692 --> 00:17:49.144
property was acquired by onerous title.

00:17:49.554 --> 00:17:53.949
D: community properties if property was acquired by gratuitous title.

00:17:54.359 --> 00:17:55.724
E: none of the above.

00:18:01.734 --> 00:18:03.803
The answer is E: none of the above.

00:18:04.463 --> 00:18:06.276
Same question, same answer.

00:18:06.436 --> 00:18:09.455
The rentals are conjugal under Article one

00:18:09.455 --> 00:18:12.474
seventeen, number three, without any only-if condition.

00:18:13.834 --> 00:18:15.114
Question 22.

00:18:15.274 --> 00:18:18.858
Fruits from exclusive property of a spouse shall form part of the:

00:18:19.318 --> 00:18:23.521
A: Exclusive property of spouse in CPG.

00:18:23.931 --> 00:18:29.648
B: CPG property ONLY if prop acquired by gratuitous title.

00:18:30.058 --> 00:18:35.882
C: ACP property ONLY if prop acquired by gratuitous title.

00:18:36.292 --> 00:18:41.967
D: ACP property ONLY if prop acquired by onerous title.

00:18:42.377 --> 00:18:43.742
E: None of the above.

00:18:49.752 --> 00:18:51.821
The answer is E: none of the above.

00:18:52.481 --> 00:18:57.217
In a conjugal partnership, all net fruits of exclusive property are conjugal.

00:18:57.377 --> 00:19:01.377
In an absolute community, the fruits follow the property: excluded

00:19:01.377 --> 00:19:05.377
property keeps its fruits, and community property's fruits are community.

00:19:05.537 --> 00:19:09.974
The test is never only if acquired by onerous or gratuitous title.

00:19:11.335 --> 00:19:12.658
Question 23.

00:19:12.818 --> 00:19:15.058
A parcel of land is not presumed

00:19:15.058 --> 00:19:17.298
to belong to the conjugal partnership if:

00:19:17.757 --> 00:19:20.232
A: it was acquired by gratuitous title.

00:19:20.642 --> 00:19:22.925
B: it was acquired by onerous title.

00:19:23.335 --> 00:19:25.490
C: it was acquired during the marriage.

00:19:25.899 --> 00:19:28.139
D: it was registered during the marriage.

00:19:28.549 --> 00:19:29.914
E: none of the above.

00:19:35.925 --> 00:19:37.994
The answer is E: none of the above.

00:19:38.654 --> 00:19:41.886
Under Article one sixteen, all property acquired during

00:19:41.886 --> 00:19:45.118
the marriage is presumed conjugal, whatever the title.

00:19:45.278 --> 00:19:50.163
A gratuitous acquisition can rebut the presumption, but the presumption still arises.

00:19:50.323 --> 00:19:52.974
C is the worst answer, since acquisition during

00:19:52.974 --> 00:19:55.294
the marriage is exactly what triggers it.

00:19:56.654 --> 00:19:57.955
Question 24.

00:19:58.115 --> 00:20:02.082
In a conjugal partnership of gains, without a marriage settlement, this

00:20:02.082 --> 00:20:05.689
property shall NOT form part of the conjugal partnership properties:

00:20:06.149 --> 00:20:09.221
A: the rentals from the exclusive property of a spouse.

00:20:09.631 --> 00:20:13.343
B: a property acquired by gratuitous title during the marriage.

00:20:13.753 --> 00:20:17.273
C: a property acquired by onerous title during the marriage.

00:20:17.683 --> 00:20:21.288
D: a property acquired by onerous title before the marriage.

00:20:21.698 --> 00:20:23.063
E: none of the above.

00:20:29.073 --> 00:20:31.462
The answer is D: a property acquired

00:20:31.462 --> 00:20:33.510
by onerous title before the marriage.

00:20:34.171 --> 00:20:37.933
What a spouse brings to the marriage as their own is exclusive

00:20:37.933 --> 00:20:41.382
under Article one oh nine, number one, however it was acquired.

00:20:41.542 --> 00:20:45.424
Rentals and onerous acquisitions during the marriage are conjugal.

00:20:45.584 --> 00:20:48.123
And B fails because the donor or testator

00:20:48.123 --> 00:20:50.662
may provide that it goes to the partnership.

00:20:52.021 --> 00:20:53.280
Question 25.

00:20:53.440 --> 00:20:56.213
If not absolutely true, the statement is false.

00:20:56.673 --> 00:20:57.697
Statement one.

00:20:57.857 --> 00:21:02.764
Property bought on installments paid partly from exclusive funds of either or

00:21:02.764 --> 00:21:07.670
both spouses and partly from conjugal funds belongs to the conjugal partnership.

00:21:08.131 --> 00:21:09.155
Statement two.

00:21:09.315 --> 00:21:13.037
A bigamous marriage falling under Article 41 may be void

00:21:13.037 --> 00:21:16.760
even if there is a judicial declaration of presumptive death

00:21:16.760 --> 00:21:20.110
and all the essential and formal requisites are present.

00:21:20.569 --> 00:21:21.700
A: both are true.

00:21:21.860 --> 00:21:23.182
B: both are false.

00:21:23.342 --> 00:21:25.817
C: No. 1 is true, No. 2 is false.

00:21:25.977 --> 00:21:28.558
D: No. 1 is false, No. 2 is true.

00:21:34.569 --> 00:21:37.812
The answer is D: no. 1 is false, No. 2 is true.

00:21:38.471 --> 00:21:41.543
Statement one is false: under Article one eighteen,

00:21:41.543 --> 00:21:44.231
it depends on when full ownership vested.

00:21:44.391 --> 00:21:46.482
Before the marriage, it belongs to the

00:21:46.482 --> 00:21:48.572
buyer; during the marriage, to the partnership.

00:21:48.732 --> 00:21:53.195
Statement two is true: even with a declaration of presumptive death, the

00:21:53.195 --> 00:21:57.287
subsequent marriage is void if both spouses acted in bad faith.

00:21:58.647 --> 00:21:59.948
Question 26.

00:22:00.108 --> 00:22:01.175
True or false?

00:22:01.335 --> 00:22:03.970
That which is brought to the marriage as his or

00:22:03.970 --> 00:22:06.604
her own, shall be the exclusive property of each spouse.

00:22:12.615 --> 00:22:13.810
The answer is true.

00:22:14.469 --> 00:22:19.248
That's verbatim Article one oh nine, number one, the conjugal partnership rule.

00:22:19.408 --> 00:22:23.632
Careful: under the absolute community, the same property would be community

00:22:23.632 --> 00:22:27.856
property, so if the item says absolute community, the answer flips.

00:22:29.216 --> 00:22:31.648
Part IV: Separation of Property.

00:22:32.408 --> 00:22:33.816
Question 27.

00:22:33.976 --> 00:22:36.995
The property regime of the spouses married in

00:22:36.995 --> 00:22:40.013
1999 will be complete separation of property if:

00:22:40.473 --> 00:22:42.777
A: no marriage settlement was executed.

00:22:43.187 --> 00:22:45.150
B: the marriage settlement is void.

00:22:45.560 --> 00:22:49.443
C: one of the contracting parties is a surviving spouse

00:22:49.443 --> 00:22:53.325
who contracted a subsequent marriage without liquidating the community property.

00:22:53.735 --> 00:22:55.122
D: all of the above.

00:22:55.532 --> 00:22:56.897
E: none of the above.

00:23:02.907 --> 00:23:04.059
The answer is C.

00:23:04.719 --> 00:23:07.712
For a nineteen ninety-nine marriage, no settlement or

00:23:07.712 --> 00:23:10.330
a void one leads to absolute community.

00:23:10.490 --> 00:23:14.816
Under Article one oh three, a surviving spouse who remarries without

00:23:14.816 --> 00:23:19.143
liquidating the previous community gets a mandatory regime of complete separation

00:23:19.143 --> 00:23:23.076
of property, which protects the heirs of the first marriage.

00:23:24.437 --> 00:23:26.272
That's all twenty-seven questions.

00:23:26.432 --> 00:23:28.512
Count your score, and look up anything you

00:23:28.512 --> 00:23:30.592
missed in the quiz bank and the reviewer.

00:23:30.752 --> 00:23:32.160
Good luck in Sir's quiz.
